#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106635} :activity/id #uuid "0211c399-b12f-5dd5-8ac4-97409f9a60bc" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048853} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106635 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107080 :tutorial/content "Let's consider a system of linear equations where the corresponding coefficient matrix is singular. For example, consider the following system and its associated augmented matrix:\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y &=-5 \\\\ -3x+6y &=15\\end{aligned}\\right. \\qquad M = \\left[ \\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 15\\end{array} \\right]\n$$\n\n\n\nTo solve the system, we can reduce $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as usual. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 15\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y &=-5 \\\\ 0x+0y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation $0x+0y = 0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx - 2y = -5.\n$$\n\nFinally, we describe the **general solution** of our system.\n\n- The first variable in our equation, $x,$ is called a **basic variable**.\n\n- The second variable in our equation, $y,$ is called a **free variable**.\n\nSolving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y),$ we get $x = -5+2y.$ So the general solution of the system is\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-5+2y \\\\ y &=y\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\nwhere the free variable $y$ is any number.\n\nIn vector form, this solution is written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5+2y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+y\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this is the equation of a straight line on a plane. Any point on this line satisfies our system of equations.\n\nIf we want to find some **particular solution** of the system, we simply choose some particular value of $y$ and find the corresponding value of $x$ on the line. For example, if we choose $y=0,$ then the corresponding value is $x=-5$ and the particular solution is $x=-5,$ $y=0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0c91df0b-40c7-5a65-9fda-5d2d6438dfa3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3153 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2b66da6f-e7b3-547c-b47a-3bfe17bbc205" :step/math-academy-id 17192 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106636}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106636 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105745 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105602 :question/id #uuid "7bfabbd2-9988-5c0e-9c17-3d430b3e7a0f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4699" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n\n$$\nM= \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 4\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\n\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-3y &=1 \\\\ 0x+0y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation $0x+0y = 0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx - 3y = 1.\n$$\n\nThe first variable $(x)$ is the basic variable, and the second variable $(y)$ is the free variable. Solving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y),$ we get $x=1+3y,$ and the general solution is\n\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1+3y \\\\ y &=y\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nwhere $y$ is an arbitrary number. \n\nIn vector form, the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1+3y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=⟨1+3y,\\,y⟩,\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "97cd9132-458b-5234-a3d2-22b633acb74c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050240}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551473} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392488} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402782} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186640785}] :knowledge-point/title "Reducing an Augmented Matrix for a Singular 2x2 System to REF and Solving the Resulting System"} :step/id #uuid "414f018d-78f3-5c78-86b8-6363d86d3a9e" :step/math-academy-id 17193 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106744}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106637 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105746 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105603 :question/id #uuid "681fce57-6be7-5907-b575-8c34b4819ae5" :question/math-academy-id "e-4700" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+5y &=4 \\\\ 4x+10y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system." :question/worked-solution "First, we set up the augmented matrix for the system:\n\n\n\n$$\nM = \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 10 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\n\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 10 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\frac{5}{2} & 2 \\\\ 4 & 10 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\frac{5}{2} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{5}{2}y &=2 \\\\ 0x+0y &=-8.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nHowever, the final equation $0x+0y=-8$ has no solution. It simplifies to $0=-8,$ which is a false statement.\n\nSo, there is no pair $x,y$ that satisfies both equations in the system. Consequently, the overall system has no solution."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "07f2ae4c-ff07-5b99-a7b0-90f460081eef" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561382} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392481} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562457}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Singular 2x2 System of Equations Using Gaussian Elimination"} :step/id #uuid "00d5123d-64a7-5878-9b67-ac7b7a2bf0a0" :step/math-academy-id 17194 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106638}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106638 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107081 :tutorial/content "Given a $2 \\times 2$ system of linear equations, we have the following possibilities for the number of solutions:\n\n- The system has exactly *one solution* if and only if the respective coefficient matrix is non-singular (meaning that the determinant is not zero). Such systems are called **consistent independent**.\n\n- The system has *infinitely many solutions* if the second row in the standard echelon form contains only $0$'s. These systems are called **consistent dependent**.\n\n- The system has *no solution* if the second row in the standard echelon form contains only $0$'s on the left-hand side and a non-zero element on the right-hand side. These are called **inconsistent** systems.\n\n![](images/16/1692fbea298339ff839cf2f14986d51af45d1ec72b2b7d0de179336bdef40c0e.png)\n\n**Note:** A system of linear equations of the form \n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}ax+by &=0 \\\\ cx+dy &=0,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\nwhere we have only $0$'s on the right-hand side, is called **homogeneous**. A homogeneous system always has at least one solution, namely, the solution $x=0$ and $y=0.$ Or, in vector form, $\\langle 0,0 \\rangle.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b885f832-9e76-5008-a764-7375b1c25e35" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3154 :tutorial/title "The Number of Solutions of a 2x2 System"} :step/id #uuid "dc00947a-2ca5-59a7-a7a8-349b27d59bf2" :step/math-academy-id 17195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106744 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107097 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, a system of equations might not have any solutions. For example, consider the following augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y &=3 \\\\ 0x+0y &=10.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nThe final equation, $0x+0y=10,$ has no solution. It simplifies to $0=10,$ which is a false statement.\n\nSo, there is no pair $x,y$ that satisfies both equations in the system. Consequently, the overall system has no solution." :tutorial/id #uuid "20c281f5-a455-581a-a7f7-fec6cfaa114e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3901 :tutorial/title "Singular Systems with No Solution"} :step/id #uuid "e6eff47b-880f-55d6-94ac-af8b9767c4fd" :step/math-academy-id 23052 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106637}}] :activity/title "Solving 2x2 Singular Systems of Equations Using Gaussian Elimination" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186106000}]}
